# Art Projects Rules

## General Guidelines
- Keep work clear, maintainable, and easy to reopen later
- Use clear names for files, folders, and network operators
- Comment tricky logic (expressions, Python, custom parameters)
- Follow best practices for the chosen medium (TouchDesigner, web, etc.)
- Prefer practical ELI5 explanations — teach the "why," not jargon dumps

## Creative Style
- Keep networks and scripts focused and single-purpose
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Prefer composition (reusable components) over one giant network
- Optimize for performance in real-time / interactive loops
- Start small: ship a working interactive loop before adding polish

## Interaction & Media
- Interactive art should let the audience affect something (motion, color, sound, structure)
- Separate inputs (sensors, mouse, audio) from processing and from final output
- Don't commit huge media dumps, crash dumps, or secrets
- Back up `.toe` / `.tox` files before destructive rewires

## Project Structure
- Organize by piece/project under this folder (e.g. `TouchDesigner/`)
- Separate creative logic from assets and exports
- Keep assets, notes, and reusable components in clear subfolders
- Each project should include README, `.gitignore`, and project-specific `.cursorrules` when useful

## Learning & Feedback
- Explain operator chains simply: what goes in → what changes → what comes out
- Test interactions thoroughly; note edge cases and performance limits
- Track progress in project `PROGRESS.md` or short notes
- Document known bugs, limitations, and "what I'd try next"
